[tool] tc-261015_2-w1-d-01-42 -> ok: [1702.08430v1] keV sterile neutrino Dark Matter
Abstract: We give an overview of the current status of keV sterile neutrino Dark Matter. Aft
[usage]
[direction] d-02 -> closed
**Completed — Build a cross-checked phenomenological map of viable sterile-neutrino parameter space by comparing recent BBN analyses with the established X-ray, relic-density, and structure-formation constraints..** Established a cross-checked phenomenological map. For keV sterile DM, minimal Dodelson–Widrow production supplying all DM is excluded by X-ray limits in arXiv:2112.00758 and is also disfavored by structure formation; nonstandard neutrino self-interactions can shift/widen the abundance-compatible mixing band, while a subdominant sterile fraction relaxes X-ray constraints. Resonant Shi–Fuller production requires a lepton asymmetry many orders above the baryon asymmetry but below CMB/BBN detectability limits; in arXiv:2306.16532, a 7.1-keV mixed cold-plus-warm benchmark retains models with at least 60 satellite subhalos, including nearly all-sterile DM at sin^2(2theta_astro) approximately 7e-10 and viable values below roughly 1e-9 (published Figs. 11–12). Because sin^2(2theta_astro)=f sin^2(2theta_tot), these are conditional on the sterile fraction f, production history, and the paper’s satellite-structure criterion; the authors state updated scattering rates could lower the maxima. The freeze-in/freeze-out scalar benchmark in arXiv:2204.08795 changes the abundance and momentum relation through a two-step Higgs-portal scalar production followed by sterile production, with a potentially thermalized hidden sector at T_phi<T, so its viability is model-dependent and must be tested against free streaming and X-rays rather than DW relations. For MeV–GeV HNLs, arXiv:2410.07343 finds BBN exclusion in an intermediate-mixing window: sufficiently large mixing causes decay before BBN, while sufficiently small mixing gives too little freeze-in abundance; the excluded region includes temporary matter domination and charged-meson, photon, and neutrino injection, with a separate CMB constraint for very long-lived states (published Fig. 1). The resulting comparison matrix is: DW all-DM—BBN generally not the controlling constraint for long-lived keV states, but X-ray/structure remove the minimal band; resonant all-DM—conditionally retained subject to lepton asymmetry, X-ray and structure assumptions; mixed cold-plus-warm—conditionally retained with f-dependent X-ray and suppression limits; NSSI or scalar freeze-in/freeze-out—potentially retained but explicitly model-dependent; MeV–GeV HNL—separate BBN-sensitive regime, not a keV-DM constraint. Source texts are stored at /Users/nramachandra/hep-genesis-testing/projects/hep-testing-research/runs/261015_205132/{2306.16532,2112.00758,2204.08795,2410.07343,1702.08430}.txt. Key published figures: arXiv:2306.16532 Figs. 11–12; arXiv:2410.07343 Fig. 1.
